# DIVERGENCE: `while` is for a condition, never a counter. A `while name < bound`
# whose body steps `name` by an integer constant is a hand-rolled `range` — so
# it is a compile error pointing at `for`, where CPython runs it fine. A counted
# loop is `for i, _ in range(n)`; `while` is for a condition that is not a count
# (an EOF drain `while chunk != b""`, a poll, `while true`), and those all
# compile — see std/http.oro and corpus/core/25_break_continue_finally.oro.
#
# This file is the rejection itself: the whole program is one counting `while`,
# and its output is the error. (A program cannot both trigger a compile error
# and run, so the allowed forms are exercised elsewhere and unit-tested.)
i = 0
while i < 10:
    print(i)
    i = i + 1
